-- title:
--   Bialgebra maps killing an augmentation ideal factor through a quotient
-- statement:
--   Let $R$ be a commutative ring and let $B$, $G_1$, $G_2$ be commutative rings carrying $R$-bialgebra structures. Let $\pi : B \to G_1$ be a morphism of $R$-bialgebras whose underlying function is surjective, and let $j : G_2 \to B$ be a morphism of $R$-bialgebras. Assume the exactness condition that the kernel of the underlying $R$-algebra map of $\pi$ equals the ideal of $B$ generated by the image under $j$ of the augmentation ideal of $G_2$, i.e. $\operatorname{ker}(\pi) = \mathrm{Ideal.map}\ j\ (\operatorname{ker}(\varepsilon_{G_2}))$, where $\varepsilon_{G_2}$ is the counit of $G_2$ viewed as an $R$-algebra map. Let $x : B \to B$ be a morphism of $R$-bialgebras such that the composite of $j$ with $x$, as $R$-algebra maps, equals the composite of the counit $\varepsilon_{G_2} : G_2 \to R$ with the structure map $R \to B$. The conclusion is that there exists a morphism of $R$-bialgebras $y : G_1 \to B$ with $x = y \circ \pi$ as bialgebra morphisms (in Lean, $x$ equals `y.comp π`).
--
--   In the language of affine group schemes this is the statement that an endomorphism of a commutative group scheme which is trivial on a subgroup factors through the corresponding quotient, written here on the level of commutative bialgebras, the exactness hypothesis recording that $\operatorname{Spec} G_1$ is the quotient of $\operatorname{Spec} B$ by the image of $\operatorname{Spec} G_2$. It is used in the study of $p$-divisible groups, specifically in the identification of a power of the Cartier dual map with a composite of Frobenius and Verschiebung.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Bialgebra_exists_eq_comp_of_comp_eq_counit_of_ker_eq_map.lean

